From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josep Maria Blasco <josep.maria.blasco@epbcn.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 14:17:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] WebAssembly port, browser: ADDRESS JDOR, drawing on the
 canvas

JDOR ("Java Drawing for ooRexx", Rony G. Flatscher), the drawing command
handler of BSF4ooRexx850, ported to the browser build: Java2D commands on
the HTML canvas, no Java involved.

    address jdor                   -- select the JDOR environment
    newImage 300 200; color red; moveTo 10 10; fillRect 100 50; winShow
    ::requires "jdor.cls"          -- set up the JDOR environment

- wasm/web-jdor.js (--pre-js): a port of JavaDrawingHandler.java
  (100.20230718) to Canvas 2D, on the main runtime thread (the worker that
  hosts the run, which never blocks): commands, aliases, results,
  canonical output (ADDRESS ... WITH OUTPUT), command input (WITH INPUT),
  ERROR/FAILURE conditions and return codes as in Java.  The objects
  commands return say what they are, JDOR objects of the browser build
  ("JDOR Image #99: 500x500, ..."), and where Java throws an exception the
  condition's description reads "JDOR (web): ...".  Images are
  OffscreenCanvases; at most once per animation frame a visible window's
  image goes to the page as an ImageBitmap.  Areas use polygon-clipping
  (MIT, wasm/web/polygon-clipping.umd.min.js), loaded on first use.
- wasm/web-jdor.cpp: the package "jdor" (JdorNewHandler,
  JdorAddEnvironment, JdorSetup) and a redirecting command handler added
  with AddCommandEnvironment; each command is passed synchronously to the
  main runtime thread (emscripten_proxy_sync_with_ctx), which may finish
  it later; Rexx variables the command needs are resolved on the
  interpreter thread; sleeping happens there too.
- interpreter/package/PackageManager.cpp: packages a WASM build links in
  through a weak hook, orx_wasm_extra_packages(), defined only by the
  browser build (NULL elsewhere); loaded on first request.
- wasm/web/jdor.cls: requiring it sets up the JDOR environment;
  addJdorHandler (as BSF4ooRexx's jdor.cls) sets up more; JdorObject, the
  Rexx side of the objects commands return ("a JDOR Image (#99)",
  toString, kind, getters such as rc~width, passed back by name).
- wasm/web/BSF.CLS (also preloaded as bsf.cls): not the Java bridge; just
  what BSF4ooRexx JDOR programs use (.bsf~new of the handler,
  bsf.importClass, BsfCommandHandler "add", bsf.createJavaArrayOf, pp, a
  few .java.lang.System properties), so they also run unchanged.
- wasm/web/ooRexx.js: run({onJdor, screen}) and run.jdorEvent(); the page
  decides how to show the windows (ooRexx-worker.js relays the messages).
- wasm/tests/web-api.py: 8 JDOR cases (30 in all).
